    "raw": "VERDICT (2026-08-05): zero-shot tagging by cosine-vs-candidates DOES NOT WORK on\nthis corpus, and the defect is the EMBEDDING SPACE, not the candidate domain.\n0 of 36 mined-domain cells produced a usable topical tag. A curated,\nuniform-length label set -- the cleanest domain possible -- scored\nprecision@1 = 0/6. Do not spend more effort on candidate-domain variations.\n\nPredecessor: ef4ad78d (shipped the machinery; its own follow-up list is largely\ninvalidated by this spike). Successor: 91e28fd0 (tuned importance pipeline).\n\n=== EXPERIMENT DESIGN (reproducible from this description) ===\n\nEmbedder: apple (Apple NLContextualEmbedding via the Swift sidecar), 512-dim,\nmean-pooled, l2-normalized. The only production embedder; `hash` was removed\n2026-08-04.\n\nSubjects: 6 todos chosen for maximum topic separation and mixed body length.\n  515fc9ea  test sqlite storage suite            87 chars    1 chunk\n  cc756c32  wt-inventory old worktrees          123 chars    1 chunk\n  a31d8f24  vec search ranking                   95 chars    1 chunk\n  a46c04b0  flac directory names from metadata  666 chars   15 chunks\n  21e3454c  ESP32 /healthz and /logs endpoints  941 chars   51 chunks\n  ef4ad78d  zero-shot semantic tagging        7,317 chars  420 chunks\n\nGold labels (stated up front so results are falsifiable):\n  515fc9ea -> automated testing / sqlite storage\n  cc756c32 -> git worktrees\n  a31d8f24 -> vector search ranking\n  a46c04b0 -> audio (flac) file/directory naming from metadata\n  21e3454c -> esp32 embedded http endpoints\n  ef4ad78d -> semantic tagging with embeddings\n\nCandidate domains:\n  A  subject n-phrases    483 -- 1..3-phrase windows from the 6 subjects only\n  B  corpus n-phrases   4,639 -- 1..3-phrase windows from all 55 todos (shipped)\n  C  corpus keywords    2,697 -- word-level 1..3-grams, all 55 todos, freq >= 2\n                                 (26,280 raw uniques; hapax-filtered)\n  D  curated labels         14 -- 2-3 word concept labels, 10 real + 4 distractors\n\nDocument granularity:\n  whole -- one vector for Summary+Body concatenated (what the shipped tagger does)\n  chunk -- one vector per 1..3-phrase window, candidate scored by MAX over chunks\n\nCorrections tried: none (raw cosine), and per-candidate z-score across all 55\ndocs (the hubness correction ef4ad78d specified and never implemented).\n\nMetrics: precision@1/@3 vs gold; score spread; count of candidates within 0.01\nof rank-1; whether rank-1 was sourced from the subject itself.\n\n=== EVIDENCE ===\n\n1. COSINE RANKS CANDIDATE LENGTH, NOT TOPIC.\n   Fixed target (flac/audio metadata), irrelevant candidate grown word by word:\n     1 word  'git'                                            +0.476\n     4 words 'git branch checkout worktree'                   +0.832\n     7 words '... inventory teardown stale'                   +0.875\n     13 words '... commit push merge rebase origin master'    +0.895\n   Relevant candidates for the same target:\n     1 word  'flac'                                           +0.645\n     2 words 'audio metadata'                                 +0.698\n     5 words 'flac audio metadata directory naming'           +0.916\n   A 13-word IRRELEVANT candidate (0.895) beats the 2-word RELEVANT\n   'audio metadata' (0.698) by +0.20. Topic wins only when it is also long.\n\n2. THE NUISANCE IS ~10x THE SIGNAL.\n   Mean cosine of each curated label to all 55 corpus docs, with its sd:\n     +0.8138  sd=0.0174  billing and invoicing      <-- DISTRACTOR, top hub\n     +0.7776  sd=0.0260  command line interface\n     +0.7685  sd=0.0207  embedded hardware\n     +0.7428  sd=0.0211  git worktrees\n     +0.7280  sd=0.0178  semantic tagging\n     +0.7150  sd=0.0251  audio file metadata\n     +0.7140  sd=0.0310  sqlite storage\n     +0.7120  sd=0.0266  automated testing\n     +0.7090  sd=0.0301  vector search\n     +0.6942  sd=0.0256  user interface design      <-- distractor\n     +0.6924  sd=0.0240  schema migration\n     +0.6913  sd=0.0304  network routing            <-- distractor\n     +0.6688  sd=0.0331  firmware updates\n     +0.6173  sd=0.0331  access control             <-- distractor\n   Between-label spread = 0.20. Within-label variation across documents =\n   sd ~0.02. The label's fixed position dominates by an order of magnitude:\n   WHICH DOCUMENT YOU SCORE BARELY MOVES THE NUMBER. And the strongest hub is a\n   distractor -- 'billing and invoicing' is closer to all 55 todos than every\n   relevant label, with the lowest variance, and takes rank 1 for 5 of 6\n   subjects.\n\n3. BARE NUMERALS ARE DEGENERATE HUBS.\n   cos('5','6') = 0.9932   cos('6','7') = 0.9941   cos('5','8') = 0.9881\n   cos('5','five') = 0.5427  cos('5','testing') = 0.5564\n   Not number semantics -- short digit strings collapse to one point. Numbered\n   lists in bodies yield bare-digit phrase windows, so chunk-max matches\n   digit-to-digit and beats every real phrase. On ef4ad78d/chunk: rank-1 =\n   1.0000 with 318 of 483 candidates within 0.01 of it, median 0.9938. The\n   entire domain is effectively one point.\n\n4. SELF-MATCH IS UNAVOIDABLE IN MINED DOMAINS.\n   Rank 1 was the subject's OWN sentence in 12/12 of the A and B cells\n   (0.985-1.000). At chunk granularity it is exactly 1.0000 -- the todo matching\n   its own phrase verbatim. Any mined domain must exclude self-sourced\n   candidates or the output is tautological.\n\n5. DOMAIN SCOPE MADE NO DIFFERENCE.\n   A (483, subjects only) vs B (4,639, whole corpus): same rank-1s, same ~0.98\n   scores, medians within 0.005. Shrinking the domain 10x changed nothing.\n\n6. SCORE COMPRESSION.\n   Domains A/B: median cosine 0.87-0.93, rank-1 ~0.98 -- thousands of candidates\n   inside a 0.10 band. Curated domain D: total spread across 14 wildly different\n   labels was only 0.06-0.26. No threshold can separate a good tag from a bad one.\n\n7. PER-CANDIDATE Z-SCORE HELPS, THEN FAILS DIFFERENTLY.\n   It correctly cancels the per-candidate length offset and produced the FIRST\n   correct answers in the whole spike:\n     515fc9ea -> 'test coverage of the tag feature'      (+1.74 sigma)\n     cc756c32 -> 'that branch and its worktree'          (+3.42 sigma)\n   But on keyword domain C it inverts into a RARITY bias, promoting dates\n   ('2026-07-20'), identifiers ('ce147239', 'sha256'), ASCII table borders, and\n   near-stopwords ('rather', 'partly', 'questions'). ~2/6 at best. Necessary,\n   nowhere near sufficient: after removing the offset only sd~0.02 of real signal\n   remains, and other artifacts dominate it.\n\n8. GRANULARITY ONLY RESCUES VERY LONG DOCUMENTS.\n   Chunking produced the single genuine hit of the curated arm -- ef4ad78d\n   (420 chunks): 'vector search' +0.9071 and 'semantic tagging' +0.8939 as ranks\n   1 and 2, both correct. It HURT the mid-size doc 21e3454c (51 chunks):\n   whole = hit@3 -> chunk = MISS, spread collapsing 0.189 -> 0.084. More chunks\n   means more chances for a hub to max out.\n\n=== SYMPTOMS (how this presents in production) ===\n\n- Tags are long sentence fragments lifted from unrelated todos.\n- Tags include bare digits, dates, file paths, ASCII art.\n- A few candidates recur across topically unrelated todos.\n- Every score is ~0.9, so no threshold separates good from bad.\n- Todos tagged with their own text.\n\nAll of these matched the real store before it was cleared: ef4ad78d's three\nautomatic tags were long fragments mined from an unrelated todo about\n\"relationship kinds\".\n\n=== CONCLUSIONS ===\n\n1. As shipped the method does not work -- 0/36, not marginal.\n2. The candidate domain is NOT the problem. Subject-scoped, corpus-scoped, and\n   word-level keyword domains all fail. This invalidates ef4ad78d follow-up #3\n   (mine word n-grams) and the \"shrink the domain\" idea as fixes.\n3. Granularity is NOT the problem, except as a rescue for very long documents.\n4. The z-score (ef4ad78d follow-up #2) is necessary but NOT sufficient.\n5. Curating the vocabulary does NOT save it. This is the decisive result: with\n   the domain perfect by construction, a distractor still beats the truth 5/6\n   times. That isolates the embedder as the defect.\n6. Apple NLCE is unfit for zero-shot topical assignment on this corpus. This\n   confirms 91e28fd0's own note (\"compressed cosine range, keys on shared\n   surface tokens over concept\") with a gold standard and numbers.\n7. Removing `hash` was correct, but switching auto-tagging to `apple` would NOT\n   have worked either. Auto-tagging staying dormant is the right state.\n\n=== NEXT STEPS (in priority order) ===\n\n1. CHEAPEST DECISIVE TEST: re-run the curated 6-subject arm (domain D, whole +\n   chunk, raw cosine, precision@1/@3) against the `st` backend --\n   sentence-transformers all-MiniLM-L6-v2, already registered hidden in\n   todo_embed._BACKENDS, selectable as --embedder st. One flag, ~2 minutes.\n   MiniLM is TRAINED for sentence-level semantic similarity rather than\n   token-level contextual embedding, so it is the single variable most likely to\n   move precision@1 off zero.\n   - If it works: auto-tagging becomes viable on a curated label set + st, and\n     the hubness z-score should be added on top.\n   - If it also fails: the conclusion generalizes from \"wrong embedder\" to\n     \"wrong architecture\", and 91e28fd0's tuned-scorer pipeline is the only\n     remaining path. Record that either way -- it is the key branch point.\n2. Whatever embedder is chosen, apply BOTH corrections before top-k:\n   per-candidate z-score across the corpus (kills the length/hub offset) AND a\n   candidate filter dropping bare numerals, 1-char tokens, dates, hex\n   identifiers and ASCII-art runs (proven artifacts, item 3/7 above).\n3. Prefer a CURATED label set over mined candidates. Mined domains additionally\n   require self-source exclusion (item 4) and still emit sentence fragments.\n4. Re-arming is automatic: `_recompute_auto_tags` stands down only because\n   `todo_embed.cheap_embedders()` is empty. Registering a cheap semantic backend\n   turns it back on -- so DO NOT register one until precision@1 is acceptable on\n   a gold set, or the corpus refills with noise.\n5. Guard the zero-vector failure mode first: `_is_vector` accepts an all-zero\n   vector as present and `cosine_similarity` returns 0.0 for a zero-norm input\n   instead of raising, so the trust-existing backfill never repairs one. 77% of\n   stored apple vectors were inert this way before 0b6b738 repaired them.\n\n=== NOTES ===\n\nExperiment scripts were session scratch and are NOT preserved; the design\nsection above is sufficient to rebuild them. Nothing in this spike wrote to the\nstore. Method summary: embed candidates once, cache, score each subject by\ncosine (whole-field vector, or max over per-n-phrase-window vectors), sort\ndescending with ties broken on candidate text ascending, take top-k.\n"
  },
